/**
 * @page vfs_limitations VFS Limitations
 *
 * The VFS offers POSIX-like semantics, it does not claim POSIX
 * conformance. This page lists the known limits: those of the file systems
 * and devices below the VFS, the scope of the VFS interfaces, and the open
 * issues.
 *
 * - @ref vfs_lim_backends
 * - @ref vfs_lim_api
 * - @ref vfs_lim_paths
 * - @ref vfs_lim_concurrency
 * - @ref vfs_lim_adapters
 * - @ref vfs_lim_issues
 * .
 *
 * @section vfs_lim_backends File systems and devices
 *
 * <b>FatFS</b>
 * - Independent opens of the same file are safe only when all of them are
 *   read-only, a writer invalidates the state cached by the other handles.
 *   Applications opening a file more than once enable @p FF_FS_LOCK, sized
 *   for the simultaneously open files and directories, conflicting opens,
 *   removals and renames are then rejected with @p CH_RET_EBUSY.
 *   Duplicated descriptors share one native handle and are not affected.
 * - Rename does not replace an existing destination, it returns
 *   @p CH_RET_EEXIST. Open files and directories must not be removed or
 *   renamed, @p FF_FS_LOCK rejects it for files only.
 * - Names follow the FAT rules, long names depend on the FatFS
 *   configuration. FAT has no owner, permission or link information and
 *   stores times with two seconds of resolution, without a time zone.
 * - FAT has no sparse files, a write after a position past the end of file
 *   writes the zeros filling the gap, in a time proportional to its size.
 * .
 * <b>LittleFS</b>
 * - Independent opens of the same file do not share their state, opens
 *   conflicting with a writer are rejected with @p CH_RET_EBUSY.
 *   Duplicated descriptors and independent read-only opens are safe.
 * - Open files can be removed and renamed, their handles are detached and
 *   data written through them afterwards is lost.
 * - There are no timestamps, owner or permission information, custom
 *   attributes are not exposed.
 * - Separate instances work on disjoint storage areas, a shared storage
 *   device needs its own synchronization.
 * .
 * <b>ROMFS</b>
 * - Contents and names are read-only, opening for writing, creating,
 *   removing and renaming return @p CH_RET_EROFS.
 * - There are no timestamps or file identities.
 * - Dynamic and compressed content callbacks synchronize their own state,
 *   the VFS does not serialize them.
 * .
 * <b>Streams</b>
 * - The names are registered statically, creating returns
 *   @p CH_RET_EROFS, removing, renaming and directory changes return
 *   @p CH_RET_ENOSYS.
 * - Sequential devices have no position and no size. A random access
 *   element is a device with one position, separate opens share it, its
 *   size comes from the backend. Writable append and truncation are
 *   rejected.
 * - Read and write ordering, terminal state and nonblocking behavior are
 *   properties of the device behind the element.
 * .
 * <b>ChibiFS and template drivers</b>
 * - ChibiFS is not implemented, its operations return
 *   @p CH_RET_ENOSYS. The template driver is a starting point for driver
 *   authors, not a usable file system.
 * .
 *
 * @section vfs_lim_api Interface scope
 *
 * - Sizes and positions are of type @p vfs_offset_t, a signed 32-bit
 *   integer, positions beyond @p INT32_MAX return @p CH_RET_EOVERFLOW.
 *   Files are limited to @p INT32_MAX bytes: a FatFS file of 2 GiB or more,
 *   written by another system, is rejected by open and stat with
 *   @p CH_RET_EOVERFLOW and its directory entry reports @p INT32_MAX, a
 *   write at the limit returns @p CH_RET_EFBIG and a write crossing it is
 *   shortened. LittleFS limits files natively to @p LFS_FILE_MAX, at most
 *   @p INT32_MAX, a write crossing it returns @p CH_RET_EFBIG.
 * - Open accepts the access mode, @p O_APPEND, @p O_CREAT, @p O_TRUNC,
 *   @p O_EXCL, @p O_DIRECTORY and @p O_CLOEXEC, other flags are rejected.
 *   Seeks support @p VFS_SEEK_SET, @p VFS_SEEK_CUR and @p VFS_SEEK_END.
 * - There are no sync or truncate operations, @p O_TRUNC at open excepted.
 *   Closing a file does not report flush errors, and the last release of a
 *   node can happen after the descriptor is closed.
 * - The opened access mode is enforced, there are no owners, permissions,
 *   umask or timestamp updates.
 * - There are no hard or symbolic links, @p openat() family,
 *   @p pread()/@p pwrite(), file locks or file system space queries.
 * - @p VFD_CLOEXEC is recorded but no VFS user implements an exec, sandbox
 *   ELF calls return to the caller.
 * - Directories are read with first and next calls, there are no
 *   @p telldir()/@p seekdir() positions.
 * - File system objects and their backends are managed by the caller,
 *   mount, unmount and format require that no operation or node is active.
 *   There is no hot removal support.
 * - Path lengths, name lengths, buffer pairs, descriptor tables and mount
 *   tables are configured bounds.
 * .
 *
 * @section vfs_lim_paths Paths and current directory
 *
 * - @c "." and @c ".." are resolved lexically, without checking the
 *   intermediate elements: @c "missing/../file" is @c "file" even if
 *   @c "missing" does not exist.
 * - The current directory is stored as a path. Through its root, the
 *   current directory and its ancestors are not renamed and the current
 *   directory is not removed, @p CH_RET_EBUSY is returned. Other roots over
 *   the same file system and direct file system calls are not checked,
 *   they can leave a stale current directory.
 * - Each root has one current directory, shared by all the users of the
 *   root. Users needing their own current directory use separate roots.
 * - The overlay driver routes paths to the mounted file systems and lists
 *   them, it is not a union file system. Directory listings are live,
 *   changes of the mounts during a listing can skip or repeat entries.
 * - Renaming across file systems returns @p CH_RET_EXDEV. Unregistering a
 *   mount does not unmount or dispose the file system.
 * .
 *
 * @section vfs_lim_concurrency Concurrency
 *
 * - Without @p VFS_CFG_USE_MUTUAL_EXCLUSION the callers serialize the
 *   operations sharing a root, an overlay or a file system.
 * - Node references protect lifetimes, not ordering: operations on the same
 *   node, including descriptors sharing it, are serialized by the callers.
 *   A seek followed by a position read is not atomic.
 * - The FatFS driver uses one mutex for all volumes, the LittleFS driver
 *   one per instance, held during the storage operations. Storage callbacks
 *   do not call the VFS.
 * .
 * See @ref vfs_arch_concurrency for the complete rules.
 *
 * @section vfs_lim_adapters C library and sandboxes
 *
 * - The Newlib bindings install no descriptor, the application installs
 *   the standard descriptors. C library @p FILE objects are not protected,
 *   a stream is not used by several threads at the same time.
 * - @p stat() and @p fstat() report the type and the size, the sandbox
 *   also a link count of 1. Sandbox directory records report inode 1.
 * - The sandbox directory records have a fixed layout shared by host and
 *   guest, both are built from the same tree. A directory read needs room
 *   for a record with a name of maximum length.
 * - Sandboxes are isolated from each other only by disjoint name spaces:
 *   each sandbox root has its own path prefix, shared content is mounted
 *   read-only. Sandboxes sharing writable directories can remove, change
 *   or rename each other's files. The host does not rename or remove the
 *   prefix directory of a running sandbox, or a directory holding the
 *   current directory of another root.
 * .
 *
 * @section vfs_lim_issues Open issues
 *
 * - No sync or truncate, close errors discarded:
 *   https://github.com/chibios-upstream/chibios/issues/639
 * - Incomplete stat metadata:
 *   https://github.com/chibios-upstream/chibios/issues/640
 * - LittleFS open files detached by removal and rename:
 *   https://github.com/chibios-upstream/chibios/issues/651
 * .
 */
